The registry as files: the consumer's build lints, without the build. `lint`
and `lock` reach no bus at all; `export` and `diff` do.

```console
$ zenctl registry --help
The registry as a document: export it, diff it, lint it

Usage: zenctl registry <COMMAND>

Commands:
  export  Export the loaded slice set as a document
  diff    Diff local `--registry` files against what the fleet serves
  lint    Run the RFC 08 §5 registry lints on a directory, as a build would
  lock    Write or update the RFC 08 §3.1 compatibility lock (registry.lock)
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

$ zenctl registry export --help
Export the loaded slice set as a document.

`--as toml` round-trips through `--registry <dir>`; `--as jsonschema` bundles the producers' served `describe` schemas (RFC 08 §7); `--as asyncapi` maps subjects to channels and procedures to operations.

Usage: zenctl registry export [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --as <TARGET>
          Output document. A foreign schema, so `--format` has no say over it: passing both is a usage error, not a silent preference

          Possible values:
          - toml:       Registry TOML — round-trippable through `SliceSet::from_dirs`
          - jsonschema: A JSON Schema bundle built from the producers' served `describe` replies (RFC 08 §7)
          - asyncapi:   An AsyncAPI 3.0 document: channels from subjects, operations from procedures
          
          [default: toml]

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl registry diff --help
Diff local `--registry` files against what the fleet serves.

RFC 08 §6: a disagreement is a finding, not an ambiguity. `doctor` judges a deployment; this just shows the two registries side by side.

Usage: zenctl registry diff [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl registry lint --help
Run the RFC 08 §5 registry lints on a directory, as a build would

Usage: zenctl registry lint [OPTIONS] <DIR>

Arguments:
  <DIR>
          The registry directory (the one a build script points at)

Options:
      --ledger <FILE>
          Deprecation ledger; defaults to `<dir>/deprecated.lock`

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl registry lock --help
Write or update the RFC 08 §3.1 compatibility lock (registry.lock).

Additive evolution and `[[deprecated]]` retirement regenerate cleanly; an INCOMPATIBLE edit (changed type/class/kind/shape on an existing path) is refused — retire and add a sibling instead. `--force` overrides, and prints every broken pin: the escape hatch is legal, silent it is not.

Usage: zenctl registry lock [OPTIONS] <DIR>

Arguments:
  <DIR>
          The registry directory (the one a build script points at)

Options:
      --force
          Rewrite pins over an incompatible edit — the loud break

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```

## `--as` is the same rule (#243)

```console
$ zenctl registry export --as asyncapi --format ndjson
? 2
error: the argument '--as' cannot be used with '--format <FORMAT>'

--as emits a foreign document format — somebody else's schema — while --format chooses among zenctl's own three renderings of a report. Drop one.

Usage: zenctl <COMMAND>

For more information, try '--help'.

```
